{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1412258-optional-rule-dangerous-violence","id":1412258,"title":"Optional Rule: Dangerous Violence","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-09-06T04:45:12-07:00","id":1412258,"title":"Optional Rule: Dangerous Violence","content":"<span style=\"font-size:14px\">Hi, everyone. After skimming a few other games and considering the general predictability of combat and violence in <i>CofD </i>games, I scribbled together some notes on a variant rule to change things up a bit. Since I'd already written it up, I figured I might as well share it on here <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><b>Optional Rule: Dangerous Violence</b></span><br /> Violence is unpredictable and frightening. This hack is an attempt to introduce that unpredictability into the core combat system of the <b>Chronicles of Darkness</b>. Every time a character is seriously hurt, there is a chance that they will suffer severe trauma or even death.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>The Rule</b></span><br /> Whenever you suffer one or more points of Lethal or Aggravated damage from a single, immediate source of harm (such as an attack, a fall, or a collision), you immediately need to roll your Stamina, subtracting the amount of Lethal or Aggravated damage inflicted. Ongoing damage, such as that from fire, the Poisoned or Bleeding Tilts, or similar effects, does not call for this roll.<br /> <br /> <b>Roll Results</b><br /> <b>Success: </b>You carry on as before, with no effect.<br /> <b>Failure: </b>The wound is crippling. You may choose to either immediately suffer a Tilt and keep going, <i>or</i> to fall unconscious and begin to bleed out. Each minute, you take another point of Lethal damage, until you are stabilised.<br /> <b>Exceptional Success: </b>You push through the damage, gaining the <i>Steadfast </i>Condition, which lasts for the remainder of the fight or action sequence.<br /> <b>Dramatic Failure: </b>The damage is critical, killing you immediately.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>Optional: Important Player Characters</b></span><br /> As Player Characters are not only more dramatically important than Storyteller Characters, but will also be exposed to a great deal of damage in an average career, consider replacing the Dramatic Failure result with the following:<br /> <b>Dramatic Failure: </b>As the results of Failure, but the damage is critical, upgrading one of the points of Lethal damage inflicted to Aggravated, or adding an additional point of damage if it was already Aggravated.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>Optional: Beaten Down</b></span><br /> Given that the Beaten Down &amp; Surrender optional rule covers some of the same ground, it\u2019s not recommended that you use both. Rather, consider applying the <i>Beaten Down</i> Tilt as a consequence of a failed resistance roll.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>Optional: Down and Dirty Combat</b></span><br /> If you resolve a conflict using Down and Dirty Combat, run it as usual, but consider the damage inflicted as part of the resolution to be eligible for this rule, potentially leaving one or both participants in a rough state.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>Notes</b></span><br /> This hack is more suitable to gritty games, in which violence is ugly and dangerous, than for games focusing on action or combat as a central part of the game. In the latter, it is important that players are able to meaningfully predict the flow of combat, and this sort of unpredictability is likely to disempower them; in the former, someone picking up a rusty iron pipe or a handgun should be cause for immediate terror.<br /> <br /> Whether or not this hack applies to supernatural creatures is up to you; arguments can be made that vampires should be immune to the standard effects of a Dramatic Failure (with a possible exception for bladed weapons), as should Prometheans. You may also wish to consider this on a case-by-case basis.<br /> <br /> <br /> EDIT: Added the rather key element of the amount of damage inflicted is a penalty to the Stamina roll.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24057,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24057-malus-black","user":"Malus Black","date":". 09-07-2020, 08:58 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24057-malus-black","name":"Malus Black","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24057_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-06T13:58:55-07:00","id":1412329,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus Black</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1412258-optional-rule-dangerous-violence#post1412258\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">After skimming a few other games and considering the general predictability of combat and violence in <i>CofD </i>games,...</div> </div> </div> </div>This could probably use some unpacking. What do you mean by &quot;the general predictability of combat and violence in the CofD?&quot; As something that's easily statistically modeled, it's predictable in one sense, but that doesn't mean any given use of the combat system isn't worth rolling because the outcomes are always going to be obvious from the start.<br /> <br /> What's your threshold for &quot;predictability&quot; here? It's hard to offer many thoughts on how a house rule will push things if the goal isn't clear.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Whenever you suffer one or more points of Lethal or Aggravated damage from a single, immediate source of harm (such as an attack, a fall, or a collision), you immediately need to roll your Stamina. Ongoing damage, such as that from fire, the Poisoned or Bleeding Tilts, or similar effects, does not call for this roll. </div> </div> </div>Considering the default damage in CofD is lethal for any armed combat (which most characters are going to use) this seem like it's going to result in a lot of extra rolling with little actual change to the predictability of a fight. More rolls does not mean less predictable.<br /> <br /> Some crude examples:<br /> <br /> One heavy statted combatant vs. a combat minimal combatant: In the RAW, they're very likely to win because they have lots of dice, a high Defense, and high damage, plus some Merit tricks to pull. Only an astronomically lucky hit from the minimal is going to do anything that matters. With this house rule... nothing really changes. Mostly things get worse for the minimal because they're getting hit more, and have low Stamina so they'll get even more Tilts stacked on the ones the heavy is already likely piling on them through high damage output alone.<br /> <br /> Two white-room even as even gets combatants: In the RAW, this is probably going to be pretty whiff heavy as the risk/reward math says to play the 50/50 odds of being the first to get a lucky big hit in, rather than trying to do a big risk big reward move. With the house rule, this is again just further reinforced, but probably even slower since even minor hits mean more rolls, and likely both sides having Tilts sitting on them making it even harder for one side to try to change things up.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Dramatic Failure: </b>The damage is critical, killing you immediately. </div> </div> </div>When is this ever really going to come up? Nobody is going to opt in to this for a Beat on a dead character, and every character has at least Stamina 1 to avoid having a Chance Die here. If Wound Penalties are supposed to apply here, then it's still only going to be an issue when a character was already extremely close to being taken out anyway. The fact that you have an alternate rule for PCs already is kind of evidence of this being the case. Why not just have that be the default?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> in the former, someone picking up a rusty iron pipe or a handgun should be cause for immediate terror. </div> </div> </div>You can't enforce &quot;terror&quot; with something like this. You can only adjust the odds to make players weight the risks.<br /> <br /> The terror is that most people in the CofD can only take 1-3 hits from even a &quot;minor&quot; weapon like a rusty pipe or small handgun without being KOed and left bleeding to death. A heavily combat invested character has the Defense to avoid more hits from average combatants, and the Health to bump up how many hits they can take from an average combatant, but they don't actually know what the strengths of who they're facing actually are. Knowing your character &quot;should&quot; win 95% of the time doesn't mean you want to run into that 5% chance they lose when you don't have to.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-07T06:18:04-07:00","id":1412423,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1412258-optional-rule-dangerous-violence?p=1412329#post1412329\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This could probably use some unpacking. What do you mean by &quot;the general predictability of combat and violence in the CofD?&quot; As something that's easily statistically modeled, it's predictable in one sense, but that doesn't mean any given use of the combat system isn't worth rolling because the outcomes are always going to be obvious from the start.<br /> <br /> What's your threshold for &quot;predictability&quot; here? It's hard to offer many thoughts on how a house rule will push things if the goal isn't clear.</div> </div> </div> </div>Good point. Basically, the way fights go, in my experience, is that it always comes down to trading blows. I hit you, you hit me, and 'round we go to another turn. You can make a pretty fair assessment, based on the dice pools and Defences in place, as to how long it will last, and who will come out on top. That's not to say that there isn't unpredictability, obviously there is, and we've probably all had a massive dice pool whiff, or two dice explode into a solid hit, but on the whole, this holds true.<br /> <br /> With this variant, any blow can be the final one, and any hit can cause a Tilt that might shift the balance of the fight. I also find that players are more likely to consider their wounds when they walk away from combat with a broken leg, than with the relatively abstract &quot;three boxes of Lethal damage&quot;, but I concede that this might be a personal matter with the people I play with.<br /> <br /> I'll also entirely agree that implementing this rule will stack the odds even more in the favour of combat-oriented characters, and also incentivise high Defence, Stamina, and damage outputs, more than neat tricks and fancy moves, which might, honestly, be less fun all around. I'm considering using this for a short campaign or two and see how it actually plays out, and if it's desirable for a certain tone or not.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> When is this ever really going to come up? Nobody is going to opt in to this for a Beat on a dead character, and every character has at least Stamina 1 to avoid having a Chance Die here. If Wound Penalties are supposed to apply here, then it's still only going to be an issue when a character was already extremely close to being taken out anyway. The fact that you have an alternate rule for PCs already is kind of evidence of this being the case. Why not just have that be the default? </div> </div> </div>Hah! Thanks for pointing that out, I completely forgot to add the bit where the damage inflicted is a penalty to the Stamina roll, and I've edited the original post to that effect. So yeah, that, plus the wound penalties, as you point out, is how to have the potential of Dramatic Failures on any attack.<br /> <br /> And a big point of this particular rule is thus: that you can't, as players, just wail on someone until they're down and then patch them up (either mundanely or with whatever supernatural abilities you have available), because you might accidentally just plain kill them. As it is, the odds of someone accidentally dying, particularly with supernatural powers in the mix, is vanishingly low, and I suppose that's what leads into your next point...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You can't enforce &quot;terror&quot; with something like this. You can only adjust the odds to make players weight the risks.<br /> <br /> The terror is that most people in the CofD can only take 1-3 hits from even a &quot;minor&quot; weapon like a rusty pipe or small handgun without being KOed and left bleeding to death. A heavily combat invested character has the Defense to avoid more hits from average combatants, and the Health to bump up how many hits they can take from an average combatant, but they don't actually know what the strengths of who they're facing actually are. Knowing your character &quot;should&quot; win 95% of the time doesn't mean you want to run into that 5% chance they lose when you don't have to. </div> </div> </div>Which is, I suppose, that the &quot;terror&quot; I'm talking about is that, once weapons come out, someone might die. It probably isn't going to (or even should) be a player character, but they might end up with blood on their hands and a corpse, or a good friend having taken a surprise bullet in the head. In a sense, it's less about shaking up the combat, per se, as it is about the consequences of it, and the players knowing that when they, or others, get to the point of applying lethal force, the consequences can mount quickly and unpredictably.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24057-malus-black","name":"Malus Black","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24057_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-07T08:35:32-07:00","id":1412440,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus Black</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1412258-optional-rule-dangerous-violence?p=1412423#post1412423\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Basically, the way fights go, in my experience, is that it always comes down to trading blows.</div> </div> </div> </div>But, ultimately, you didn't change this, you just added another thing to track while characters trade blows at each other. While the chance of death goes up significantly, what's going to be far more common is lots of characters with Tilts making combat even slower as they lose even more dice from their attacks. Or worse, players feeling touchy about who gets which Tilts when, as Tilts are not equal in penalty. One character getting Knocked Down is going to be far better off than one that gets hit with Stunned. Knocked Down and Stunned also resolve quickly, where others might persist past combat (and even then that's a ST call). There's a lot of room for group acrimony here considering how drastically different the outcomes of the same dice rolls can be based on the ST's decisions.<br /> <br /> This also significantly risks returning to the far less fun &quot;predictable&quot; nature of 1e combat: whoever wins Initiative wins because they'll alpha strike you before you can do anything. Even if you survive you'll get hit with a Tilt before you can even do anything, two if they make it a called shot. If Knocked Down or Stunned is in there... they can just keep doing it until they win too.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I also find that players are more likely to consider their wounds when they walk away from combat with a broken leg, than with the relatively abstract &quot;three boxes of Lethal damage&quot;, but I concede that this might be a personal matter with the people I play with. </div> </div> </div>I think you might want to look as some of the work people are doing trying to convert the CofD stuff to the Storypath system since it sounds like you'd appreciate it's damage system more (even if the defeat Storypath rules are very non-lethal).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Hah! Thanks for pointing that out, I completely forgot to add the bit where the damage inflicted is a penalty to the Stamina roll, and I've edited the original post to that effect. So yeah, that, plus the wound penalties, as you point out, is how to have the potential of Dramatic Failures on any attack. </div> </div> </div>This kinda jumps from &quot;gritty&quot; into &quot;grimdark&quot; as far as tone goes. Most characters having a 1/10 chance of dying from a minimal success roll to hit them is pretty high and runs a significant risk of having players just detach from caring about characters since they'll just die so easily. All the potential drama of, &quot;Oh no, I accidentally killed someone I was just trying to hurt!&quot; doesn't last if the odds are so high you start to wonder what the point of having an ER around is. This is a very high penalty for a straight Stamina roll.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And a big point of this particular rule is thus: that you can't, as players, just wail on someone until they're down and then patch them up (either mundanely or with whatever supernatural abilities you have available), because you might accidentally just plain kill them. </div> </div> </div>Eh? If you have the supernatural powers of healing to get away with this.... why not? Each supernatural type has its own themes to explore and ways to handle this. If my Life mage has the power to turn a human into a frog, why on Earth should they have to worry about being able to torture some hapless Sleeper by using magic to make sure that even when beaten senseless, they never die? The consequences for a mage doing that lay elsewhere.<br /> <br /> The idea that normal humans in the CofD can just beat people up until their full of lethal damage &quot;safely&quot; is fairly absurd. If the players wail on someone with 7 Health, and end up dealing 10 L, that person has 4 minutes to live, and it takes an extended Dex + Medicine roll that requires 10 successes total just to keep them from dying, and each roll takes a minute... even with a great medic around... four rolls to get 10 successes is by no means guaranteed.<br /> <br /> If the players are wailing on someone until they're down and don't have supernatural powers, that target's most likely going to die... assuming the PCs didn't intent to just let them die anyway.<br /> <br /> Of course, the odds of someone dying with supernatural powers in the mix vastly depends on what the supernatural powers does...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In a sense, it's less about shaking up the combat, per se, as it is about the consequences of it, and the players knowing that when they, or others, get to the point of applying lethal force, the consequences can mount quickly and unpredictably. </div> </div> </div>However it's just quickly, not unpredictably.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}